p=Path('/workspace/sites/olympiad-atlas');new=[]
def add(y,n,r,t,tags,s,reason,solution=None):
 url=f'https://web.evanchen.cc/exams/USAMO-{y}-notes.pdf'
 new.append(dict(id=f'us-usamo-{y}-{n}',country='United States',competition='USAMO',year=y,number=str(n),statement=s,solution=solution or f'[View Evan Chen’s worked solution notes]({url}).',sourceUrl=url,solutionUrl=url,solutionKind='original-exposition' if solution else 'source-link',topic=t,tags=tags.split('|'),difficulty=r,ratingReason=reason,reviewStatus='individually-assessed',notes='Statement restated in original wording; mathematical argument individually checked. Linked author-maintained notes are not labelled official.'))
add(2022,5,7,'Combinatorics','Binary constructions|Supports|Pigeonhole principle|Monotonicity',r'Call $f:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ essentially increasing if $f(s)\le f(t)$ whenever $s\le t$ and both $f(s),f(t)$ are nonzero. Find the least integer $k$ such that every list $x_1,\ldots,x_{2022}$ of real numbers can be represented as $x_n=f_1(n)+\cdots+f_k(n)$ for $1\le n\le2022$, using $k$ essentially increasing functions.','A decreasing negative list forces distinct nonempty supports, giving a power-of-two lower bound. Attainment requires a binary-block construction with successively larger scales and a compensating coordinate; verifying monotonicity across every block is the substantial part.',solution=r'''The answer is $11$. More generally, $N$ prescribed values require $\lceil\log_2(N+1)\rceil$ functions.

For necessity, choose $x_t=-t$. For each position let $S_t=\{i:f_i(t)\ne0\}$. The supports are nonempty. If $s<t$ had $S_s=S_t$, summing monotonicity over this common support would give $x_s\le x_t$, a contradiction. Hence $N\le2^k-1$.

For sufficiency, pad the list with zeros to length $2^k-1$. Choose $M\ge1$ bounding all absolute values and $B>2M+1$. Divide the positions into consecutive blocks of lengths $1,2,4,\ldots,2^{k-1}$. In block $j$, label the positions in order by $r=2^{j-1}-1,\ldots,0$. At the position $t$ labelled $r$, set $f_i(t)=2^{i-1}B^j$ for $i<j$ whose corresponding binary digit of $r$ is $1$, and set the other $f_i(t)$ for $i<j$ to zero. Set $f_j(t)=x_t-rB^j$, and set $f_i(t)=0$ for $i>j$.

The sum is $x_t$ because the binary weights add to $rB^j$. Function $f_j$ first appears in block $j$, where its successive values increase: each step raises $-rB^j$ by $B^j>2M$. Its last value there is at most $M$. In each later block it is either zero or the positive constant $2^{j-1}B^h$, and these constants increase with the block index $h$. Thus each function’s nonzero values are increasing. Define all functions to be zero off the prescribed integer positions. Taking $k=11$, since $2022\le2047$, completes the construction.''')
add(2021,2,6.5,'Combinatorics','Planar graphs|Reversible states|Walks|Extremal constructions',r'A finite planar graph is drawn with three edges incident to every vertex. Starting at a vertex and choosing an outgoing edge, a visitor follows the edges, alternately turning left and right at successive vertices, beginning with a left turn. She stops upon first returning to her starting vertex. Over all such graphs and walks, what is the largest possible number of arrivals at any one vertex?','A directed turn determines both the future and past, so no turn can repeat before the return. Pairing each turn with its reverse sharpens the naive six-state bound to three. A planar cubic construction attaining three must then be verified; the bound depends on reversibility, not merely finiteness.')
add(2020,3,6.5,'Number theory','Quadratic residues|Two-to-one maps|Products modulo primes',r'Let $p$ be an odd prime. An integer is a quadratic nonresidue modulo $p$ if it is not congruent to any square. Let $A$ consist of the integers $1\le a<p$ for which both $a$ and $4-a$ are quadratic nonresidues modulo $p$. Find $\prod_{a\in A}a\pmod p$.','Introduce the complementary set where both entries are nonzero quadratic residues. The quadratic map t↦t(4−t), with one exceptional input removed, is two-to-one onto that set. Multiplying the paired roots and cancelling the auxiliary product gives the answer. Discovering the map is the key insight.')
add(2020,5,7,'Combinatorics','Polynomial interpolation|Double counting|Subset bounds',r'Call a finite set $S$ of plane points overdetermined if $|S|\ge2$ and some nonzero real polynomial $P$ of degree at most $|S|-2$ has $P(x)=y$ at every $(x,y)\in S$. For each $n\ge2$, find the greatest number of overdetermined subsets that an $n$-point set can have while not itself being overdetermined.','Uniqueness of a polynomial through enough points shows that two overdetermined subsets missing different points would force their parent set to be overdetermined. A descending double count then bounds every subset size. Matching this bound and respecting the nonzero-polynomial condition require an explicit construction.')
add(2020,6,7,'Algebra','Rearrangement inequality|Averaging|Variance|Permutations',r'Let $n\ge2$, $x_1\ge\cdots\ge x_n$, and $y_1\ge\cdots\ge y_n$ be real numbers with $\sum_i x_i=\sum_i y_i=0$ and $\sum_i x_i^2=\sum_i y_i^2=1$. Prove $\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^n(x_iy_i-x_iy_{n+1-i})\ge\frac2{\sqrt{n-1}}$.','Average the dot product over every permutation of the y-values. Its mean is zero and its second moment is explicitly computable; a range-versus-variance bound then gives the required gap. Rearrangement identifies the extreme permutations. The probabilistic reinterpretation is the central, non-obvious step.')
